SM(605,{"n":"Hebrews 12.1-29","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Hebrews 12.1-29.mp3","d":3628.7,"s":[[0.32,"Come and study the Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.96,"Father Stephen holds a PhD in biblical studies, and it's an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[20.56,"Okay, so we'll go ahead and get started."],[22.48,"And uh when we get started in just a moment, we're gonna be picking up at the beginning of St."],[27.52,"Paul's Epistle to the Hebrews, chapter 12."],[32.4,"And as I mentioned when we ended last time, um this isn't a totally random chapter break."],[41.92,"Uh there kind of needed to be a chapter break somewhere in here."],[46.16,"This is as good a spot as any, but uh we really are gonna be picking up kind of in media race again uh here."],[54.88,"So um, because with this chapter begins with. sort of the summary conclusion of chapter 11"],[64.2,"If you say, well, why didn't they just put the chapter break right after that?"],[67.16,"Well, because the part right after that is building directly on that."],[70.6,"So there's not like there is not within chapters 11 and 12 really they're really kind of a unit, but that would be a huge unit."],[83.24,"So there needs to be a break somewhere."],[85.8,"This is as good a place as any."],[88.2,"And so we went ahead and used that as an opportunity to end last time."],[94.92,"But so uh by way of review, and so for a review of the epistle as a whole and for the introduction, by the time anyone hears this, you'll be able to listen to the the intro in first Bible study."],[108.4,"Uh, but so just to catch us up with in terms of chapter 11 and where the current discussion is going."],[114.48,"Remember the lead into chapter 11 in chapter 10."],[117.46,"Because this is a as we've said several times, this is sort of a rhetorical piece more than a letter."],[122.92,"That so it it builds, the argument builds."],[126.2,"Um chapter 10 had been talking about, it was one of the several passages that talks warns against apostasy."],[134.53,"About standing firm in the faith, in the face of adversity, um and not turning away or falling away."],[143.57,"And so then we saw chapter 11 last time uh is predominated by examples, starting all the way back in Genesis 1, verse 1, as we saw, sort of going through a good chunk of Genesis and then getting into Moses a little bit, and then sort of saying, Well, we don't have time to like go through the entire rest of the Old Testament, so we'll wrap it up here."],[170.81,"But all of the examples, the key thing here was the idea that all of these great saints of the Old Covenant of the Old Testament had this promise, the promise of Christ set before them."],[187.17,"And they spent their lives on this earth, this life and this earth, being faithful to God, even though they didn't see with their own eyes the fulfillment of that promise."],[198.31,"And this is was set up in parallel to the promise of Christ's return that we've received."],[206.65,"With the full expectation that most of us, definitely most Christians from 2,000 years ago to now, lived and died not seeing Christ's return, not seeing justice restored, not seeing the new heavens and the new earth with their own eyes."],[224.33,"Nevertheless, they lived their lives faithfully."],[227.78,"And that faithfulness, that continuing to live faithfully, is as opposed is what's opposed to falling away, turning away from Christ, specifically falling away, sort of becoming in love with and entranced by the things of this world, rather than remaining focused on the hope that's been set before us."],[248.08,"So St. Paul is setting up that we are in an analogous position."],[253.34,"And so he, of course, was saying this to people in you know the early 60s AD."],[259.8,"Saying you're in an analogous position to our fathers from the old covenant."],[265.54,"We could today add to that the saints who have lived between, well, St."],[270.42,"Paul himself, and the saints who have lived from his day until ours, in the present time, have also remained faithful, throughout their lives, despite not receiving those promises with their own eyes."],[284.21,"Um, but did receive at the time of their passing a good testimony, when they entered into Christ's kingdom."],[294.65,"And so that's really was the thrust."],[296.73,"And so it was example after example after example of that, in the lives of these various figures, how they remained faithful in the face of adversity."],[306.87,"Um I think that's probably I know that was brief, but I think that's probably enough review to get us into where we're picking up here uh in chapter twelve."],[316.31,"Uh so unless there are any leftover questions or comments or further horror. stories about European driving, uh, we'll go ahead and pick up in uh St"],[327.95,"Paul's Epistle to the Hebrews, chapter 12, uh verse 1."],[337.07,"Therefore, we all know what that means."],[340.71,"Therefore, we also, since we are surrounded by so great a crowd a since we are surrounded by so great a cloud of witnesses, let us lay aside every weight and the sin which so easily ensnares us, and let us run with endurance the race that is set before us."],[360.6,"So what St."],[363.08,"Paul is saying here is these people aren't just dead and gone now."],[366.12,"That's part and parcel of what he's been saying"],[370.7,"We saw ever since the beginning of Hebrews that he's been talking about, it's very much been in the context of this uh context of Christ being enthroned at the right hand of God."],[384.88,"The angels who we saw in the first couple of chapters, in heaven, the saints."],[390.51,"These saints of the old covenant who have now gone and joined them."],[394.88,"That so this is not just uh where we're headed."],[401.46,"If we if we endure to the end and if we remain faithful, but they're looking on, and supporting us."],[409.98,"They're continue to be alive, they're not sort of dead and gone, and it's just sort of a moral, you know, role model kind of thing."],[417.66,"But there's this active involvement."],[420.98,"Well, how do we know that's what he means?"],[422.74,"Because he says, since we're surrounded by them."],[427.03,"He doesn't say thinking about them or just remembering them."],[430.81,"He says we're surrounded by them."],[432.73,"And since we are, let's take courage and strength from them and push on, in our own race."],[440.24,"They've run theirs and finished."],[442.49,"So now we're running ours, and we need to show endurance, and we need to set aside every weight and the sin. which so easily ensnares us"],[451.71,"The things of this world."],[453.57,"That might trip us up, that might distract us."],[457.03,"We need to push those aside, not try to carry them with us."],[461.37,"Because if you try and run a race carrying a VCR or a TV, it's not going to go so well."],[467.46,"I don't know if you were looting in this example or what, but uh it's not gonna go so well if you try to run with with weights."],[477.46,"Verse 2, looking unto Jesus, the author and finisher of our faith, who for the joy that was set before him endured the cross, despising the shame, and has sat down at the right hand of the throne of God."],[490.28,"So he says this, pattern we see in these Old Testament saints."],[496.93,"Christ is sort of the predominant example of that pattern."],[502.39,"In fact, Christ is the reality of this, and what we're really seeing in their lives is Christ."],[508.73,"When we look at their lives and their faithfulness, we're really seeing, really seeing Christ, because Christ, of course, endured the cross."],[519.43,"Didn't turn aside, didn't turn back, remained faithful, remained steadfast, and now is enthroned at the right hand of the Father."],[529.37,"And so when we emulate these uh saints of the old covenant, what we're really doing is emulating Christ."],[539.35,"We're emulating what it looks like for a human like us to become like Christ."],[549.35,"For consider him who endured such hostility from sinners against himself, lest you become weary and discouraged in your souls."],[559.75,"So hostility is uh it's really more like abuse."],[565.45,"Because Christ was, of course, murdered by them."],[568.34,"This is just talking about they said mean things, they canceled him on Twitter."],[572.72,"This is he was he was beaten, he was mocked, he was spat on, he was died a literally excruciating death."],[580.98,"Crucifixion was so horrible and painful, they invented a word to describe the painfulness of it."],[587.62,"Excruciating. Um when we're thinking of getting weary and discouraged, with what we face."],[600.38,"He's saying, remember what Christ faced."],[604.06,"Quit whining. Quit being a snowflake."],[606.76,"Because we've, well, as we're about to see here in verse 4, you have not yet resisted to bloodshed, striving against sin."],[615.82,"Yes, you're struggling with sin."],[618.31,"But you haven't been tortured, you haven't been executed, you haven't been."],[624.09,"So compared to what Christ endured, our afflictions so far are light."],[628.5,"So if you're thinking about under those relatively light afflictions of giving up, think about Christ."],[634.44,"Think about the martyrs of the old covenant, the people of Rason and too."],[637.74,"The people who we just mentioned."],[640.4,"And and uh take courage from that."],[646.64,"Verse 5, and you have forgotten the exhortation, which speaks to you as to sons."],[653.76,"My son, do not despise the chastening of the Lord, nor be discouraged when you are rebuked by him."],[659.36,"For whom the Lord loves, he chastens, and scourges every son whom he loves."],[667.56,"And uh that's from Proverbs."],[670.36,"There are not a lot of quotes from Proverbs, believe it or not, in the New Testament, but this is one of them."],[675.32,"Um but this is an important one."],[679.21,"Because St. Paul here, by quoting this here in this context, is saying that the sufferings we endure in this life, even if they come from the wicked, even if they come from people who hate God."],[694.21,"What those sufferings actually are, or at least can be for us, is God's chastening, God's disciplining of us."],[701.09,"That's what chastening is talking about."],[703.99,"And so it's comparing that to the way a father or mother disciplines their children."],[711.45,"When you're a little kid, I don't know if anybody even still does this anymore, but when I was a little kid and I got swatted on the butt, I did not enjoy it."],[720.54,"And I thought my parents were mean and horrible people who hated me and just liked hurting me."],[727.44,"And wanted me to be miserable and never do anything fun."],[730.33,"When you're a child, you think that."],[732.7,"Hopefully, as you're an adult, you realize, like, oh no."],[740.96,"So St. Paul here is making this analogy between a father and son to the way in which God deals with us."],[755.88,"And when we're kids."],[758.7,"When we're kids and we receive chastening or correction or rebuke."],[763.36,"All of the language that's used here in translation, of course, scourges is kind of uh weighted because it sounds like uh physical punishment per se."],[774.4,"But the idea here is that this is God is a father correcting us as his children."],[779.52,"When we're kids and our parents uh correct us uh or punish us, we take it as being just this horrible meanness, you know our parents uh won't let us go and do what we want because they're mean and they hate us and uh don't want us to be happy and uh why are they doing this to us you know uh hopefully uh when we when we get a little older and more mature uh we look at the correction we receive from our parents and say oh they didn't want me to play in traffic because they didn't want me to get hit by a truck right they didn't want me to uh they didn't want me to uh shove a fork in a light socket, you know, not because it wasn't an interesting science experiment, but because they didn't want us to get electrocuted."],[828.32,"And so we sort of understand."],[831.36,"Uh and St."],[833.56,"Paul here is sayinging that we as mature adults need to apply that same understand to the way in which uh we understand the sufferings uh that come into our own lives."],[848.16,"Because in the same way that that a young child does that with their parents and sort of blames them and accuses them, and why are you making this happen?"],[857.28,"Why are you uh being so mean to me?"],[860.16,"Do you not really love me?"],[862.0,"Uh we think that's silly as adults when when children do it to their parents, but we're prone to do the same thing with God."],[870.88,"We're prone to have that same kind of sense of entitlement that a child has."],[875.28,"You know, I deserve to do what I want, I deserve to eat the whole bag of. Oreos"],[880.52,"I deserve to get whatever I desire right at that moment and never have to deal with anything I don't want to deal with or do anything I don't want to do or be told no."],[890.76,"We often take that attitude, even as adult humans, with God, uh, with the the troubles and difficulties and suffering that come into our life."],[901.08,"And so St."],[902.28,"Paul is trying to here express the meaning."],[906.81,"The meaning that at least can be derived from those things when they come into our life."],[912.19,"That we can know uh that God is God is allowing these things to come into our life to enable us to grow in order to enable us to become more like him, to experience his love and his grace and his comfort, and to continue to grow and become more like Christ."],[934.09,"Which is his goal."],[935.45,"This is why St."],[936.73,"Paul here emphasizes whom the Lord loves, he chastens, and every son whom he receives, he scourges."],[951.93,"The way it typically is, is that we look at people who have a lot of material things who are successful in their job or have a big family, or always have a brand new car and live in a big house and and have all these things. that we say, oh well, God's blessing them"],[971.41,"And if we're struggling in any way, then we start to wonder, if not out loud, at least in our heads, you know, why is why is God doing this to me?"],[983.22,"Doesn't God love me?"],[984.18,"Doesn't God care about what I'm going through?"],[986.34,"And St. Paul here with this quote uh from Proverbs is is really turning that on its head."],[994.68,"It's really turning it on his head."],[997.08,"Um, that the the people whom God truly loves are the people with whom he has this relationship of fatherhood and who he's trying to help grow uh into the likeness of Christ. who he wants to share his life with, who he's trying to bring to maturity and completion and perfection, as we've talked about before"],[1019.68,"So those things happening is actually the sign that God loves us."],[1024.01,"Because if if you want a sure sign, if you see a parent who never, ever, ever disciplines their child, who just lets their child do whatever they want, lets them go run in traffic, lets them go and stick the fork in the light socket."],[1036.99,"Just ignores them."],[1037.87,"We wouldn't say, oh, that's the parent who loves their child the most."],[1041.63,"We'd say that's the parent who loves their child the least."],[1044.35,"They don't care about their child."],[1046.91,"They're not concerned about them."],[1049.07,"They're not trying to raise them right."],[1050.91,"They're not trying to raise them to be a successful adult."],[1053.49,"And operate in the world."],[1055.91,"And that kid's going to have horrible consequences from that when he does get out into the world"],[1061.07,"He's going to be dealing with that for a long time."],[1063.49,"That's not what love actually looks like."],[1066.89,"What love actually looks like is a parent who deeply cares about their child and is trying to help them become the person who God created them to be."],[1075.93,"And that's how God deals with us."],[1080.25,"So in verse 7, if you endure chastening, God deals with you as with as with sons."],[1087.85,"For what son is there whom a father doesn't chasten?"],[1091.79,"So he's saying, you know, just as I had to give an example a minute ago of, you know, if there was a parent who literally never disciplined their child."],[1101.61,"Well, St. Paul can't think of one either."],[1103.55,"So he's making that point."],[1104.91,"He's like, really, do you know of anybody who's a child in a family who their parents exercise no discipline whatsoever?"],[1114.64,"Absolute zero. There's always some."],[1116.81,"There's always some limits."],[1118.41,"There may not be enough limits, but there are limits."],[1121.77,"There may not be enough structure, but there's some kind of structure."],[1125.74,"And so he's hammering this point home."],[1128.32,"And notice, notice how this works."],[1130.16,"We've talked about how Hebrews is really this sort of a sermon."],[1134.74,"And so what do we have?"],[1135.86,"We have this scripture quote for Proverbs that we just read, and now St."],[1140.18,"Paul is sort of breaking it down and explaining right and up and applying it he's saying homiletics means or a homily that means to say the same thing right and so Saint Paul is now saying the same thing as Proverbs was saying but applying it uh to his hearers right so what that means is if you're enduring chastening if you're going through difficulties in life right that means God's dealing with you as as one of his sons right as an heir to the promise right because every every son receives receives discipline from their uh from their father and then the corollary in verse eight but if you are without chastening of which all have become partakers then you are illegitimate it then you are illegitimate and not sons right and so again this is not him asserting that oh all you people out there who aren't suffering all you people out there who who are having a relatively good time of life God doesn't love you right because he as he just said in the verse before what son is there who is right and as he says in this verse if you are without chastening of which all have become partakers."],[1217.36,"So this is a hypothetical."],[1219.33,"St. Paul is saying, hypothetically, if your life was quote unquote perfect, the way you would like it to be if we asked you."],[1227.37,"If it was just like that, then just like the child who instantly receives everything he wants when he wants it and is allowed to do whatever he wants, whatever he wants to do it, that hypothetical person, that would be a sign that God didn't love them and didn't care about them."],[1244.93,"But of course, that's a hypothetical person, as as St."],[1248.37,"Paul makes clear here, because uh all have part partake of the discipline of God."],[1255.66,"All receive it."],[1257.1,"Not everyone reacts to it the same way, but all receive it because God does love everyone and does want everyone to come to salvation and the knowledge of the truth."],[1267.08,"But just as with uh with children and families, children can react different ways to discipline."],[1276.76,"Children can react to discipline by becoming rebellious and angry and embittered and hating their father, or they can receive discipline in another way that helps them to grow and to flourish and to become stronger and wiser."],[1291.53,"And and more like their father."],[1296.97,"And so that's sort of the second variable."],[1299.29,"But St. Paul is saying to everyone here, everyone the sufferings in this life, everyone has some measure of suffering in this life."],[1307.85,"And so St"],[1308.06,"And the meaning it has will depend on how you receive it."],[1312.38,"Paul wants us to receive it in the way that a child with a healthy and good relationship with their father receives discipline."],[1325.26,"Furthermore, we have had human fathers who corrected us and we paid them respect."],[1333.1,"Shall we not much more readily be in subjection to the father of spirits and live?"],[1338.59,"So, you know, we all had human fathers."],[1341.55,"They were all human."],[1342.85,"Which means they were sinners, they were fallible."],[1345.93,"They didn't always do the right thing."],[1347.93,"They didn't discipline us perfectly."],[1349.89,"You know, sometimes they were too lax."],[1352.09,"Sometimes they were too harsh."],[1354.03,"Some human fathers are even outright abusive."],[1357.04,"They were all human."],[1358.6,"But regardless of the fact that they were imperfect and didn't always know what was best for us and didn't always do the right thing and sometimes did sinful things."],[1367.24,"We still paid them respect."],[1369.41,"We still, we still gave them the honor that they were due."],[1379.5,"Because we were obedient to them and uh uh check for anybody here who isn't a parent or of the age of a parent yet."],[1390.22,"Parents don't always know what they're doing."],[1392.8,"Most parents are trying to be good parents, uh, but they don't always know what they're doing."],[1400.1,"They're sort of trying their best."],[1402.6,"And so if we as children were obedient to our parents who are trying their best, just trying their best."],[1409.41,"In order for us to try to grow up successfully, then how much more God who isn't just trying his best, God who knows everything, God who is never wrong, God who always applies everything in the right measure."],[1424.21,"How much more should we trust him?"],[1427.17,"How much more should we be willing to be obedient to him, because we know that what he's doing in our lives is absolutely 100%, 100% of the time in our best interest?"],[1440.45,"So, how much more then should we be willing to follow to follow the discipline we uh we receive and the commandments we receive from him."],[1451.45,"Verse 10 for they indeed for a few days chastened us as seemed best to them, but he for our profit, that we may be partakers of his holiness."],[1461.15,"So this is exactly what we were just saying."],[1463.73,"So for this short period of time, our human parents did the best they could."],[1469.7,"But God disciplines us always for our profit, always for our benefit, never wrong, so that we can ultimately become partakers of his holiness, so that we can become like him, so that we can become formed after his image and share his life with him."],[1491.26,"Verse 11. Now, no chastening seems to be joyful for the present, but painful."],[1497.02,"Nevertheless, afterwards, it yields the peaceable fruit of righteousness to those who have been trained by it."],[1504.56,"So, yeah, nobody likes discipline, when it happens."],[1508.51,"Nobody's happy about it."],[1509.95,"I've never met anyone in the military who really wants to go back to boot camp."],[1514.93,"It's just nostalgic for being in boot camp and wants to go back there and go through it again."],[1520.02,"Because it's that is extreme and harsh discipline."],[1523.32,"When you're in intensive training like that, or athletes who are in intensive training, you know, um, they may want to go back and relive their glory days out on the field, but they don't really want to go back and just do the training part."],[1536.97,"Because it's difficult and it's hard."],[1538.81,"And when you're a kid and you're being punished, it's like the world's coming to an end."],[1543.26,"But uh, as St."],[1546.3,"Paul here says, it's never pleasant when it's happening."],[1549.55,"But but if you're trained by it, if you receive it as discipline in the positive sense to live a more disciplined life."],[1560.21,"And to be shaped and trained and formed by it, then in the long run, it yields these good fruit."],[1568.05,"What St. Paul here calls the fruit of righteousness."],[1571.25,"It causes this transformation and change in our life."],[1575.06,"Which is more than worth going through the discipline that's so unpleasant at the time."],[1583.78,"And so St."],[1584.5,"Paul is applying this same thing to our lives."],[1587.38,"The sufferings and struggles and difficulties that come into our life are not pleasant when they're happening."],[1594.66,"He's not diminishing that in any way."],[1597.7,"We go through dark times, we go through very difficult times."],[1602.14,"But in the same way, depending on how we receive those difficulties and those struggles and those hard times that don't last forever."],[1611.5,"That's the promise of the resurrection, that it won't last forever."],[1616.14,"If we receive those in a way that we're shaped by them and formed by them and trained by them, then they're going to bring forth fruit in our life later on that are worth the difficulties we have to endure."],[1634.74,"Verse 12 Therefore strengthen the hands which hang down and the feeble knees and make straight paths for your feet, so that what is, but a uh uh kind of an odd metaphor this is another metaphor for what he's talking about, for talking about how we how we receive these things."],[1659.11,"So um if uh he talks about um the hands that hang down and the feeble knees."],[1667.65,"This is this is someone who's been exerting themselves."],[1670.63,"This is someone who's been struggling, someone who's been striving towards something, and now they're starting to weaken."],[1677.29,"He's saying, so we for if you're starting to weaken, you need to strengthen yourself, and why."],[1682.53,"Well, the way it's translated here in the New King James, what's lame may be not be dislocated, but rather be healed."],[1688.79,"So this is for example, if someone is running a distance race, and say their hip starts to hurt."],[1697.0,"There are two ways that can go."],[1700.1,"Either the person can kind of adapt and struggle through it, and it can end up being healed, you know, after some discomfort and some pain, or it can go the other way and become dislocated, and then you can't run at all."],[1714.22,"And so that's a it's a metaphor, again, for how we how we receive pain."],[1720.36,"How we receive pain, how we receive difficulty."],[1723.61,"This is meant to be encouragement."],[1725.51,"To strengthen ourselves and to sort of struggle on when we're in these difficulties and these difficult periods, to continue to push on and continue to struggle."],[1735.45,"Rather than giving in and being broken by it."],[1740.97,"Verse 14, pursue peace with all people and holiness, without which. no one will see the Lord"],[1752.13,"So this is right saying now in a more practical way."],[1758.1,"So he gives this metaphor."],[1760.12,"Strengthen your hands, strengthen your knees, strengthen your joints."],[1763.89,"Stick to it."],[1764.85,"Well, how do you do that, St."],[1767.17,"Paul? How do how do how am I supposed to?"],[1769.91,"What does that practically mean?"],[1771.27,"So that's what he's getting to now here at verse 14."],[1773.91,"Is he's gonna begin to give us some practical examples."],[1778.25,"Of how to strengthen ourselves in the struggles that we face."],[1782.39,"Rather than being broken by them."],[1784.79,"So the first one here is to pursue peace with all people."],[1788.09,"When we're going through difficulties, when we're struggling, when we're suffering."],[1793.5,"One of the ways in which that can cause us to break is it can cause us to become angry and bitter, and in that anger and bitterness, lash out at other people."],[1804.51,"And break our relationships with others."],[1808.61,"Break our love for our neighbor because of because of the suffering."],[1814.77,"And so St."],[1815.97,"Paul is here saying one of the ways then to strengthen yourself when you're suffering is to consciously pursue peace with everyone when you're going through those periods."],[1826.71,"Now it notice it says pursue peace."],[1828.97,"You you're you can't force someone else to be at peace with you."],[1831.61,"You can only be at peace with them"],[1837.14,"You can only pursue it."],[1840.04,"They have to participate to be at peace."],[1842.44,"But if you're pursuing peace, that means you're not lashing out."],[1846.92,"You're not blaming others, you're not uh taking out your own anger and frustration uh at other people."],[1856.54,"And then so the second piece in verse 14 is also holiness, without which no one will see the Lord."],[1864.0,"So he's talking again about the glorious appearing, Christ's return."],[1868.64,"If you want to stand before Christ's judgment seat, as he's been talking about already here in Hebrews, be able to do that with a clear conscience, you've got to do that through holiness."],[1879.82,"So why are these paired together?"],[1881.82,"Well, one of these is in terms of other people."],[1884.31,"Peace with other people."],[1885.35,"Holiness has to do with ourselves."],[1887.61,"Because the another way we can break when we're going through suffering and difficult times is that we can lose sight of our holiness personally."],[1901.63,"So we begin to take out, we can begin to sin, essentially, that we allow the suffering to drive us into anger and bitterness and hate, and then that expresses itself as sinful, as other other. sinful actions that aren't necessarily directly directed toward people"],[1924.87,"Um where we act out in different ways."],[1929.11,"Um this is again a practical thing."],[1934.23,"This is a so if you're pursuing peace and you're pursuing holiness, then you're not going to be going in the other direction."],[1941.27,"You're not going to be allowing the pain and the difficulty to take you uh down that other road."],[1949.91,"Verse 15: looking carefully lest anyone fall short of the grace of God, lest any root of bitterness springing up cause trouble, and by this many become defiled."],[1960.21,"So St. Paul again. this is another one of these things of preaching, is that sometimes he'll s sort of say the same thing repeatedly, as we've seen throughout St"],[1968.41,"Paul's Epistle to the Hebrews, um, over and over again."],[1973.29,"He'll sort of say the same thing two or three different ways."],[1976.11,"To try and make sure he's communicating."],[1977.71,"So this is we just had the positive of what to pursue."],[1981.31,"This is now sort of the negative, this is the what to avoid."],[1984.28,"So this is the the flip side, the mirror image."],[1986.62,"So we look carefully lest anyone fall short of the grace of God, which would be parallel to the not pursuing holiness and therefore seeing the Lord."],[1995.68,"This would be the idea that that you would instead uh not be steadfast and um and fall short."],[2006.56,"And uh this is talking about people who are striving to see the Lord"],[2010.89,"So I could throw in another sorry Calvinists here in terms of uh falling short of the grace of God."],[2016.49,"Um, but also then the opposite, as we said, of pursuing peace with all people is lest any root of bitterness spring up and cause trouble, and by this many become defiled."],[2029.5,"So uh the opposite of pursuing peace with others is allowing bitterness to take root within us, and then that goes out and affects all of our relationships with other people, and so sin, uh, we've talked a bunch before about how in the old testament sin is often. talked about as sort of like a disease"],[2053.14,"Um this is one of the ways that it's contagious."],[2057.15,"So I'm I'm suffering, I'm struggling, I allow bitterness to take root in my heart, and then because of that bitterness, I lash out at other people."],[2067.33,"And so now I've been unkind, I've been spiteful toward other people, that then affects their mood."],[2076.39,"That then affects, you know, how they view the world, our relationship breaking down, then affects their other relationships, and it can kind of spread, and it can spread throughout a community like a Christian community, like a church, it can spread through a workplace, it can spread through a family."],[2093.45,"And so uh this is sort of showing what happens if we don't make it a point in these times to pursue peace."],[2103.89,"Verse 16, St."],[2105.17,"Paul's gonna give another example."],[2107.09,"Lest there be any fornicator or profane person like Esau, who for one morsel of food sold his birthright."],[2115.09,"For you know that afterward, when he wanted to inherit the blessing, he was rejected, for he found no place for repentance, though he sought it diligently with tears."],[2132.05,"So notice Esau is already described as a fornicator and profane person."],[2138.13,"This is is we we tend to the figures like Esau, and this a whole thing, but you can go back and listen to that Bible study in Romans 9, um, in in a very stripped down way."],[2150.01,"But but of course, St."],[2151.21,"Paul knows the whole story of Jacob and Esau."],[2153.85,"And Esau, long before the episode recounted here, uh, Esau was a fornicator in that he went and married multiple uh Canaanite wives that upset um his family."],[2169.1,"And so profane is the opposite here of holy."],[2173.23,"So he's he's not pursuing personal holiness."],[2176.45,"Obviously, if he's a fornicator."],[2178.45,"Um, and so that that is already true of him."],[2182.23,"So Esau is here being used in as a as an example of uh what we saw in verse 15."],[2190.07,"The person who's not pursuing peace and who's not pursuing holiness."],[2194.36,"And so we see then in this example, he, for that one morsel of food for that one uh bowl of pottage, uh sold his birthright."],[2204.98,"So the inheritance that he was to receive, he wa had the right of firstborn."],[2211.41,"Because he came out, remember, a few moments before uh his twin brother uh Jacob did."],[2218.21,"And this was the birthright in terms of the promises made to Abraham, his uh his grandfather."],[2228.41,"And so this was sort of everything."],[2230.41,"This was the this was the promise of salvation."],[2232.75,"Now, this doesn't mean when Esau sold his birthright, this doesn't mean that he lost his salvation."],[2238.89,"He traded his salvation because, of course, there's the rest of the story."],[2243.21,"And St. Paul was just as aware of how the story of Esau ends with him being reconciled, him actually seeking peace with Jacob and being reconciled."],[2252.65,"So Esau later repented on the whole, in terms of his life and his own personal salvation."],[2260.59,"But the example that St."],[2262.67,"Paul is using here is that he traded that birthright, that firstborn status, in order to gratify his desires in a moment."],[2272.32,"In one particular moment."],[2274.37,"And so this is an example of being caught in a moment, a moment suffering."],[2280.71,"He was suffering the passion of hunger."],[2284.13,"He was suffering, he was in a moment of uh a moment of weakness."],[2290.45,"And what did he do."],[2291.99,"Rather than strengthening himself, rather than pursuing holiness and pursuing peace, he was willing to trade the most valuable thing in the world, for a bowl of soup."],[2303.8,"Why is this an example?"],[2306.84,"The importance of the example here is that that we' were're prone to make the same kind of exchanges and not really realize making it."],[2316.74,"So especially when we're suffering, especially when we're struggling, when we're dealing with stress, when we're dealing with difficulties in our life, we're prone to do whatever we feel like we need to do in that moment in order to get past that moment."],[2334.44,"To get past that moment of struggle."],[2337.84,"And making that choice can be very dangerous."],[2342.4,"Cause for a lot of people, uh, that choice could be drugs or alcohol or sexual immorality or any number of other sinful things that will just get me from this moment where I'm struggling and things are hard, you know, get me through a few more moments."],[2364.81,"And firstly, none of those solutions include this bowl of soup."],[2369.45,"It's not like, you know, this was the greatest bowl of soup in the history of mankind."],[2373.05,"It's not like Esau ate this bowl of soup and never needed to eat again for the rest of his life."],[2379.02,"Um it didn't solve any problems or fix anything."],[2383.68,"But in going down that road, he gave up everything."],[2388.48,"This is this argument is very much parallel to uh what Christ is saying when he tells us, you know, uh, what does it profit a man to gain the whole world and lose his soul?"],[2405.93,"That that the most precious thing in the world needs to be the most precious thing to us."],[2413.49,"Our our knowledge of God, our salvation, our becoming like Christ."],[2417.83,"Christ needs to be the most precious thing in our life."],[2421.03,"And if we're willing to trade him for whatever we feel like we immediately need in the moment to get past, the mo the momentary sufferings that we're dealing with."],[2432.25,"Because again, they all have an end."],[2434.01,"None of them last forever."],[2436.31,"If we're in Christ. then we're capable of losing everything"],[2442.43,"And then the second half of what Christ said, what will a man give in exchange for his soul is then what he's talking about with Esau finding no room for repentance, even though he sought it with tears."],[2454.75,"Now, this isn't saying he couldn't repent, he couldn't be saved, because of course that's exactly what happens in the story of Esau in Genesis, and St."],[2461.55,"Paul knew that."],[2462.43,"But this seeking it with tears refers to, the the the again, the actual story of Esau, where after he had sold the birthright, remember uh that uh Rebecca, Jacob's mother, sort of conspired with him, uh, put the the fleece on his arms and everything, so he could go into his father Isaac and get the blessing, receive that blessing of the the firstborn."],[2491.49,"And he did receive it."],[2493.98,"Which was which was just because Esau had given it to him."],[2498.24,"And then immediately after that, Esau went into his father's tent, and we're told there that he began to weep."],[2506.08,"That's what St."],[2507.04,"Paul's referring to here about him seeking it with tears."],[2510.69,"That uh Esau then realized, oh man, that was stupid."],[2516.07,"I made a stupid choice."],[2517.83,"I made a bad bargain."],[2520.68,"But in terms of that particular bargain, in terms of getting the birthright back, in terms of getting the firstborn status back, it was too late for him."],[2524.52,"And that's what that's what Isaac told him"],[2531.28,"Isaac said, I've already given it to your brother."],[2533.44,"I can't. It's done."],[2535.52,"What's done is done."],[2537.36,"And so, as we've seen as a theme running through Hebrews, uh, in terms of uh Christ's uh glorious appearing, in terms of Christ's judgment seat, there is a point Hebrews has been emphasizing where it's too late."],[2553.77,"Where there's no takebacks."],[2556.27,"And while any given time in our life, when we betray Christ to go after something in the moment for some sinful thing of the world, uh, for any one. of those, there is repentance open to us"],[2572.23,"God wants us to repent, wants us to return, wants us to be forgiven and healed."],[2577.83,"Eventually, there's a point of no return."],[2580.47,"Eventually, there's a point where you've gained the whole world but sold your soul."],[2586.79,"And then there's nothing you can give once you're past that end point."],[2591.99,"There's nothing you can give to buy it back."],[2594.17,"Just like there's nothing Esau could give to get the birthright back."],[2600.73,"So verse 18."],[2603.13,"For you have not come to the mountain that may be touched, and that burned with fire, and to blackness and darkness and tempest, and the sound of a trumpet. and the voice of words, so that those who heard it begged that the word should not be spoken to them anymore"],[2620.45,"For they could not endure what was commanded."],[2623.09,"And if so much as a beast touches the mountain, it shall be stoned or shot with an arrow."],[2628.69,"And so terrifying was the sight that Moses said, I am exceedingly afraid and trembling."],[2636.13,"So what we have here is very clear reference back to Mount Sinai."],[2642.74,"So Israel, the first, you know, Israel at first, they've been brought out of Egypt."],[2648.86,"They've come through the wilderness."],[2650.7,"On the first Pentecost."],[2653.03,"They come to the mountain, Mount Sinai, in order to receive the covenant."],[2656.79,"To receive the covenant from God"],[2663.63,"And these are all descriptors, calling us back to that."],[2667.9,"It was a mountain that may be touched."],[2669.52,"It's a physical mountain."],[2671.41,"Physical mountain in the world, in the desert."],[2673.73,"And it was burning at the top with fire."],[2675.57,"And it was surrounded by blackness and darkness and tempest."],[2678.79,"Clouds, storm up at the top."],[2680.31,"We read about that."],[2681.03,"There's thunder and lightnings and darkness at the top of the mountain."],[2684.79,"And the sound of a trumpet."],[2686.48,"And they heard a voice."],[2687.94,"And when they heard the voice, remember the Israelites freaked out."],[2692.32,"Because you come into the presence of the holiness of God and you immediately understand just how not holy we are all."],[2699.2,"And so they said to Moses, Don't let God talk to us anymore"],[2700.06,"And so they did too."],[2705.66,"We can't stand it."],[2706.62,"We can't bear it."],[2707.92,"We can't bear it."],[2709.2,"You go up there."],[2710.58,"You go up there and talk to him, and then you come down and talk to us."],[2713.86,"We needed an intermediary."],[2715.38,"We're too scared."],[2716.18,"We're too frightened."],[2718.38,"And this commandment was given that's quoted here."],[2721.6,"That as so much as a beast touches a mountain."],[2723.62,"Your goat gets loose and wanders up onto the mountain."],[2726.96,"The goat has to be killed for touching the mountain."],[2730.02,"And the reason it says it has to be stoned or shot with an arrow is you're not allowed to go onto the mountain to get it."],[2735.63,"So you've got to do it from a distance."],[2738.72,"and And even Moses, who went up there, as is quoted here said that he was exceedingly afraid trembling."],[2746.08,"So Moses had that same experience, of his own sinfulness in the face of God's holiness."],[2752.3,"So those are the terms in which the Torah describes that first Pentecost."],[2758.48,"When Israel was brought together as a people and given given the old covenant."],[2763.52,"We've seen these comparisons between the old and new covenant again and again and again here in Hebrews."],[2770.52,"Um, so that's how sort of terrifying and serious, it was."],[2776.04,"That's how high the stakes were."],[2778.34,"We're just talking about with Esau."],[2779.79,"The sort of high stakes."],[2780.19,"high stakes But that's how the were then."],[2785.21,"That's not the mountain we've come to, though, as we get to uh verse 22."],[2794.17,"But you have come to Mount Zion and to the city of the living God, the heavenly Jerusalem, to an innumerable company of angels, to the general assembly and church of the firstborn, who are registered in heaven, to God the judge of all, to the spirits of just men made perfect, to Jesus, the mediator of the new covenant, and to the blood of sprinkling that speaks better things than that of Abel."],[2819.74,"So we've come to something even more serious."],[2824.06,"We've come to Mount Zion, the place where God dwells"],[2828.15,"Now notice this isn't referring to the physical temple in Jerusalem."],[2833.51,"How do we know?"],[2834.71,"Remember, it was a mountain you can touch."],[2837.75,"So we're contrasting a mountain you can touch."],[2840.09,"And we've also already seen that the earthly sanctuary was a recreation or an image of the heavenly sanctuary."],[2848.65,"This is talking about that heavenly sanctuary."],[2851.29,"This is talking about what mo Moses and only Moses got a glimpse of on top of the mountain."],[2859.69,"We've now come there."],[2862.26,"We're not down at the base of the mountain with the Israelites, terrified."],[2866.1,"We've now come up the mountain."],[2868.43,"And we've come to Mount Zion, the city of the living God."],[2872.29,"The heavenly Jerusalem, where there is an innumerable company of angels, because where God is, there's the angels."],[2878.77,"Remember, we talked about all the divine counsel language about the angels at the beginning of Hebrews."],[2884.05,"And now as we're coming to the end, we're summing sort of summing some of this up."],[2888.45,"To the assembly and sure, general assembly and church, they're trying to deal with words that are synonyms."],[2894.71,"To the congregation and the assembly."],[2897.45,"The gathering of the firstborn who are registered in heaven."],[2901.46,"So this is sort of all of the heavenly beings, all the divine beings who are in heaven."],[2907.32,"To God the judge of all."],[2909.0,"To the saints"],[2909.18,"To God the Father, to the spirits of just men made perfect."],[2914.68,"And remember Hebrews 11, we were just talking about sort of the old covenant people who are then made perfect with us."],[2921.37,"That's why this just men made perfect is important."],[2924.66,"So there's there's a as we talked about there in Hebrews 11, there is this glancing reference to the understanding of the harrowing of Hades."],[2933.32,"That now it's not just the angels, but it's also human saints."],[2939.1,"Who are also there and part of this congregation."],[2943.18,"To Jesus, the mediator of the new covenant."],[2945.6,"So there's God the Father, and there's Christ as there."],[2949.17,"And remember the new covenant, the blood of sprinkling."],[2950.61,"covenant. Remember at the old down there at the base of that mountain, they were sprinkled with blood"],[2957.42,"We talked about the oath taking that went on there."],[2961.1,"Uh, and now it's the sprinkling of the blood of Christ."],[2965.87,"Uh, that that brings us into this new covenant."],[2972.27,"And the blood speaking better things than that of Abel."],[2976.03,"Remember, after Cain killed Abel, God went to Cain and asked him where his brother was, and he famously said, Am I my brother's keeper?"],[2984.11,"And God said, Your your brother Abel's blood cries out from the ground."],[2988.85,"That that blood cried out for vengeance."],[2991.22,"Christ's blood does not call out for vengeance against the people who killed him."],[2996.64,"Including us. It speaks a better word."],[2999.13,"It call cries out for the opposite, for mercy and for forgiveness and for healing and for purification."],[3006.39,"So this is what we've approached."],[3008.76,"Much greater. We've approached this much greater thing."],[3012.26,"We've been brought into the way only a select few prophets in the old covenant were."],[3017.54,"We've been brought into uh the family and the household and the city and the dwelling, the congregation, the assembly of God."],[3027.83,"Therefore, even though there's not the word therefore, verse 25, see that you do not refuse him who speaks."],[3036.03,"For if they did not escape who refused him who spoke on earth, much more shall we not escape if we turn away from him who speaks from heaven."],[3044.76,"So uh down at the bottom of that mountain, when they had the blood sprinkled on them, they all said, Oh, everything that God has commanded us, we will do."],[3054.36,"And we know darn well that uh they didn't do none of it."],[3059.38,"So, uh pardon the double negative."],[3061.64,"They didn't they didn't keep it at all."],[3064.73,"Or they didn't escape, meaning what?"],[3066.81,"Well, remember that whole generation that was sprinkled with the blood, that whole generation, minus Moses, uh Joshua, and Caleb, minus those three people, that whole generation that was at the bottom of the mountain and got sprinkled with the blood and received the covenant, died in the desert."],[3083.83,"So that's what it means by did not escape."],[3086.59,"They took that oath, they were sprinkled with that blood."],[3090.45,"And when they then went back on it, when they turned back on it, um there were consequences."],[3099.7,"And so then if we now receiving this much greater word, Christ Himself."],[3109.28,"Receiving this much greater blessing, this much greater calling."],[3114.96,"If we turn back, if we don't follow through, if we don't remain faithful, how much worse will it be for us?"],[3122.13,"And this is another one of those sorry Calvinist passages that's all through Hebrews."],[3126.53,"The whole thrust of Hebrews is talking to Christians, followers of Christ, telling them not to turn back, not to fall away, not to grow weak, not to break under the pressures they face."],[3140.21,"That's the whole thrust, as we've seen through the whole book."],[3143.22,"So the whole perseverance of the saints idea, the whole once saved, always saved idea, just is not compatible with the book of Hebrews."],[3152.84,"Makes no sense with the book of Hebrews."],[3154.6,"There would be no need to preach this homily if that was what St."],[3160.44,"Paul believed. Because if he believed that, if St."],[3163.8,"Paul were a Calvinist, he would believe that all of the elect will be saved."],[3168.98,"And anybody who sort of washes out along the way wasn't elect in the first place."],[3174.2,"And if they weren't elect in the first place, then he couldn't talk them into salvation."],[3181.04,"Um, this is not evangelism."],[3184.8,"Our Calvinist friends will say, well, we don't know who the elect are, so we have to evangelize everybody."],[3190.66,"But Hebrews is not an evangelistic homily."],[3194.9,"This isn't a sermon that that St."],[3196.98,"Paul preached out in the public square calling people to believe in Christ."],[3200.82,"This is him talking to Christians."],[3205.14,"And over and over and over and over again, his central point is that just as there were Israelites who fell away, there can be Christians who fall away, who have legitimately received Christ, who have legitimately received the heavenly spirit, who have received the sacraments, who have uh been been brought into Christ, and who will then suffer a much worse condemnation if they fall away, as is just being expressed here again."],[3241.26,"That's that's just simply what Hebrews very clearly says."],[3248.82,"Verse 26, whose voice then shook the earth."],[3253.83,"Then at when the voice spoke at Mount Sinai."],[3257.45,"The mountain shook, the ground shook, then shook the earth."],[3260.73,"But now he has promised saying, Yet once more I shake not only the earth, but also heaven."],[3267.37,"Now, this yet once more indicates the removal of those things that are being shaken, as of things that are made, that the things which cannot be shaken may remain."],[3279.42,"So I grew up in Southern California."],[3284.46,"You have to take California state history at one point in elementary school."],[3290.06,"I forget which year it was, whether it was third grade or fourth grade."],[3294.98,"Somewhere in there, we took California state history."],[3298.1,"And one of the things we always did was go on the field trip to go and pan for gold."],[3302.27,"When we were learning about the gold rush."],[3304.43,"And when you pan for gold, you take sort of a flat pan, you scoop up a bunch of silt from the bottom of the river, and you kind of shake it back and forth."],[3313.57,"And as you shake it back and forth, the dirt and stuff that are lighter sort of fall out of the pan."],[3321.09,"And as you shake it, then if there is any gold in the pan, which of course there never was any real gold in the pan uh for us kids."],[3329.09,"Um, but uh any gold in the pan, because gold is so heavy, would remain."],[3335.03,"And it would therefore stand out all the more."],[3337.75,"You would have found it un,covered. it by this process"],[3341.31,"So of course, sifting anything, draining pasta."],[3344.93,"Whatever metaphor you want to use."],[3346.47,"Where you shake something, and by shaking it, you sort of sift it out, you winnow it out."],[3352.08,"And so this is talking about uh the fact that there is a last judgment coming."],[3362.22,"That that everything, the whole creation is going to be shaken, and there's a lot that is going to fall away."],[3370.88,"And whereas gold will remain."],[3372.62,"And so, in light of that judgment, again, that coming judgment, this is why it is so important that we stand firm and remain faithful to Christ. and not fall away"],[3388.34,"Verse 28, therefore, since we are receiving a kingdom that cannot be sit shaken, let us have grace by which we may serve God acceptably, with reverence and godly fear, for our God is a consuming fire."],[3403.4,"So the kingdom we're receiving cannot be shaken."],[3407.0,"It's one of those things that's going to stay."],[3409.57,"The promise that we're receiving is something that is going to stay, that is going to remain."],[3414.95,"When all the things of this world, those unimportant things, those things that might distract us, those things we might be tempted to trade our faithfulness to Christ for, like that bowl of soup we were talking about."],[3429.65,"Those things that are going to be shaken and are going to fall away."],[3433.22,"We need to focus then on receiving grace by which we may serve God."],[3440.98,"How does this work?"],[3441.86,"Well, remember, as Orthodox Christians, grace is God acting."],[3445.78,"Grace is God doing things."],[3448.18,"Grace is God acting in the world."],[3451.43,"And so grace is the means by which we serve God, meaning when we cooperate with what God is doing in the world, then we're both serving God and receiving grace, and we're transformed by it."],[3465.46,"So God is seeking to establish. peace in the world"],[3468.62,"When we seek to be at peace with those around us, as we were talking about, we're participating in that, and that transforms us."],[3476.08,"We're serving God and we're transformed by grace."],[3480.26,"And we become a little more gold and a little less dross, a little less garbage."],[3486.31,"Um it's going to be washed away."],[3489.03,"And that that takes place across across the spectrum, of peace, of love, of justice or righteousness, of uh joy, of um all of our faithfulness."],[3507.26,"We we act in accordance with what God is doing in the world, and we are serving him by doing that, and we are transformed by his grace uh to make ourselves ready to enter into the kingdom."],[3522.15,"And then verse 29, because our God is a consuming fire, this is a similar but slightly different metaphor."],[3528.81,"We went from sifting to like purifying metal."],[3532.49,"When you purify metal, when you purify gold, you find that chunk of gold while you're panning for gold, you then take it and you put it in a furnace and melt it down, and all the garbage that's mixed in with the gold, in the big nugget, all the dirt and garbage and junk burns away."],[3552.16,"And what you have left is pure gold."],[3555.54,"So this is this is uh that is that is uh justice here, and the and the judgment is both a threat and a promise."],[3565.45,"The threat is that if we've traded all the gold that God has given us for garbage, then we're gonna get burnt up."],[3574.97,"When the sifting happens, we're gonna be left with nothing."],[3579.37,"But it's a promise in that if we are living in God's grace and we are serving God and we do remain faithful, then when that judgment comes, all that's gonna happen is that the garbage that's still in us is gonna get burned away."],[3594.33,"And the pure gold. gold that we've struggled and been faithful and that God has produced in us, that is going to stay"],[3602.85,"And it's going to remain forever."],[3606.29,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[3613.65,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[3619.01,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":20.56,"lv":2,"h":"Where we pick up: Hebrews 12","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Starting <b>Hebrews 12</b> - not a random chapter break</li><li class=\"d1\">had to be a break somewhere; <b>\"as good a spot as any\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">picking up <i>in medias res</i> - chapter opens with the <b>summary conclusion of ch. 11</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Chs. <b>11 and 12</b> are really a <b>unit</b> - would be a huge unit</li><li class=\"d1\">part right after the summary builds directly on it</li><li class=\"d0\">Intro to the epistle: see the <b>first Bible study</b> on Hebrews</li></ul>"},{"t":108.4,"lv":3,"h":"Review of chapters 10-11","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Hebrews = <b>rhetorical piece</b> more than a letter - the argument <b>builds</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Ch. 10</b>: one of several passages <b>warning against apostasy</b></li><li class=\"d1\">stand firm in the faith in the face of adversity; don't turn or fall away</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Ch. 11</b>: examples from <b>Genesis 1:1</b> on, a good chunk of Genesis, a bit of Moses, then \"no time for the rest\"</li><li class=\"d1\">key: all these saints had the <b>promise of Christ</b> set before them</li><li class=\"d1\">spent their lives faithful without <b>seeing it fulfilled</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Parallel to us: <b>promise of Christ's return</b></li><li class=\"d1\">most Christians for 2,000 yrs lived and died without seeing <b>justice restored</b>, new heavens and earth</li><li class=\"d1\">faithfulness = the opposite of <b>falling away</b> - being entranced by this world</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul to people in the <b>early 60s AD</b>: analogous position to the fathers of the old covenant</li><li class=\"d1\">also saints between Paul's day and ours - received a <b>good testimony at their passing</b></li></ul>"},{"t":337.07,"lv":2,"h":"Hebrews 12:1 - Cloud of witnesses","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Therefore\"</b> - \"we all know what that means\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>12:1</b>: surrounded by a <b>great cloud of witnesses</b>, lay aside every weight and the sin that ensnares, run with endurance</li><li class=\"d0\">The saints are <b>not just dead and gone</b></li><li class=\"d1\">ties to Hebrews from the start: Christ <b>enthroned at God's right hand</b>, angels in ch. 1-2, saints in heaven</li><li class=\"d1\">old covenant saints have <b>joined them</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not just where we're headed - they're <b>looking on and supporting us</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not a mere \"moral role model\" - <b>active involvement</b></li><li class=\"d0\">How do we know? He says <b>\"surrounded\"</b>, not \"thinking about\" or \"remembering\"</li><li class=\"d0\">They've <b>run and finished</b>; now we run ours</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Weights</b> = the things of this world that trip or distract us</li><li class=\"d1\">push them aside - \"try running a race carrying a <b>VCR or a TV</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":477.46,"lv":3,"h":"12:2-4 - Looking unto Jesus","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>12:2</b>: <b>author and finisher</b> of our faith; for the joy set before him endured the cross, despising shame, sat down at the right hand</li><li class=\"d0\">Pattern seen in the OT saints - <b>Christ is the predominant example</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christ is the <b>reality</b>; in their faithfulness we're really seeing Christ</li><li class=\"d1\">emulating them = <b>emulating Christ</b> - what it looks like for a human to become like him</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>12:3</b>: consider him who endured <b>hostility from sinners</b></li><li class=\"d1\">hostility = really <b>abuse</b>; he was murdered</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"mean words\" or <b>\"canceled on Twitter\"</b> - beaten, mocked, spat on, <b>excruciating</b> death</li><li class=\"d1\">crucifixion so painful they <b>invented a word</b>: \"excruciating\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Quit whining. Quit being a <b>snowflake</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>12:4</b>: you have not yet <b>resisted to bloodshed</b></li><li class=\"d1\">struggling with sin, yes - but not tortured or executed</li><li class=\"d1\">compared to Christ, our afflictions are <b>light</b>; take courage from Christ and the martyrs of the old covenant</li></ul>"},{"t":646.64,"lv":2,"h":"12:5-6 - Chastening from Proverbs","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>12:5-6</b>: \"My son, do not despise the <b>chastening of the Lord</b>\" - whom the Lord loves he chastens, scourges every son</li><li class=\"d0\">Quote is from <b>Proverbs</b> - not many Proverbs quotes in the NT; <b>an important one</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's point: <b>sufferings in this life</b>, even from the wicked, even from those who hate God</li><li class=\"d1\">can be <b>God's chastening</b> = disciplining us</li><li class=\"d1\">compared to how a <b>father or mother disciplines</b> children</li><li class=\"d0\">Father-son analogy for how God deals with us</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Scourges\" is weighted - sounds like physical punishment</li><li class=\"d1\">idea = <b>a father correcting his children</b></li></ul>"},{"t":711.45,"lv":3,"h":"The child's view of discipline","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">As a kid swatted on the butt: parents <b>\"mean and horrible\"</b>, hated me, wanted me miserable</li><li class=\"d0\">As adults we see: they didn't want me to <b>play in traffic</b> or <b>shove a fork in a light socket</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"not because it wasn't an interesting science experiment\" - didn't want us <b>electrocuted</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Mature adults must apply the same understanding to <b>sufferings in our lives</b></li><li class=\"d0\">We do the child's thing with God: <b>\"Why are you being so mean? Do you not love me?\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">same <b>sense of entitlement</b> - \"I deserve to eat the whole bag of <b>Oreos</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">never told no, never doing what we don't want</li><li class=\"d0\">Meaning to draw from suffering: God allows it so we <b>grow</b>, become <b>more like him</b></li><li class=\"d1\">experience his <b>love, grace, comfort</b> - goal: <b>likeness of Christ</b></li></ul>"},{"t":951.93,"lv":3,"h":"Who God actually loves","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Usual view: <b>material things</b>, successful job, big family, new car, big house = \"<b>God's blessing them</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">If we struggle: \"Doesn't God love me? Doesn't God care?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's quote <b>turns that on its head</b></li><li class=\"d1\">those God truly loves = relationship of <b>fatherhood</b>, helping them grow into <b>Christ's likeness</b></li><li class=\"d1\">wants to share his life, bring to <b>maturity, completion, perfection</b></li><li class=\"d0\">So suffering is actually a <b>sign God loves us</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Parent who <b>never disciplines</b> - lets kid run in traffic, fork in socket</li><li class=\"d1\">we'd say that parent loves the child <b>least</b>; not raising them to be a successful adult</li><li class=\"d1\">kid gets <b>horrible consequences</b> out in the world</li><li class=\"d0\">Real love = parent who <b>deeply cares</b>, helps child become who God created them to be → how God deals with us</li></ul>"},{"t":1080.25,"lv":2,"h":"12:7-8 - Sons and discipline","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>12:7</b>: if you endure chastening, God deals with you <b>as with sons</b>; what son is not chastened by his father?</li><li class=\"d1\">can Paul think of a family with <b>zero discipline</b>? Always some limits, some structure</li><li class=\"d1\">\"may not be enough limits, but there are limits\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Hebrews is a <b>sermon</b> - note how it works</li><li class=\"d1\">scripture quote (Proverbs), then Paul <b>breaks it down and applies it</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><i>homiletics</i> / homily = say the same thing, applied to hearers</li><li class=\"d0\">So enduring difficulty = God treating you as <b>a son, an heir to the promise</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>12:8</b>: without chastening, of which all are partakers, you are <b>illegitimate</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not saying \"you people having a good life, God doesn't love you\"</li><li class=\"d1\">it's a <b>hypothetical</b>: perfect life, everything on demand = sign God <b>doesn't care</b></li><li class=\"d1\">but <b>all partake</b> - God loves everyone, wants all to come to <b>salvation and the knowledge of the truth</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Second variable: <b>how you react</b></li><li class=\"d1\">child can become <b>rebellious, angry, embittered</b> - or grow, flourish, become <b>wiser and like the father</b></li><li class=\"d1\">meaning depends on how you receive it - like a child with a <b>healthy relationship</b> with their father</li></ul>"},{"t":1325.26,"lv":2,"h":"12:9-10 - Human fathers and the Father of spirits","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>12:9</b>: human fathers corrected us and we paid respect; shall we not more readily submit to the <b>Father of spirits</b> and live?</li><li class=\"d0\">Human fathers were <b>sinners, fallible</b></li><li class=\"d1\">sometimes too lax, sometimes too harsh, some <b>outright abusive</b></li><li class=\"d1\">still gave them <b>honor</b> and obedience</li><li class=\"d0\">Parents \"don't always know what they're doing\" - <b>trying their best</b></li><li class=\"d0\">God <b>knows everything, is never wrong</b>, applies everything in the right measure</li><li class=\"d1\">how much more should we <b>trust and obey</b> - 100% of the time in our best interest</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>12:10</b>: they chastened us <b>\"for a few days\"</b> as seemed best to them; he for our <b>profit</b></li><li class=\"d1\">human parents did the best they could, for a short time</li><li class=\"d1\">God always for our benefit → <b>partakers of his holiness</b></li><li class=\"d1\">become like him, <b>formed after his image</b>, share his life</li></ul>"},{"t":1491.26,"lv":2,"h":"12:11 - Peaceable fruit of righteousness","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>12:11</b>: no chastening is joyful at the time, but painful; afterward <b>peaceable fruit of righteousness</b> to those trained by it</li><li class=\"d0\">Nobody likes discipline when it happens</li><li class=\"d1\">never met anyone in the military who wants to <b>go back to boot camp</b></li><li class=\"d1\">athletes in intensive training - nostalgia for glory days, not the <b>training</b></li><li class=\"d1\">kid being punished: \"world's coming to an end\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Never pleasant - Paul <b>doesn't diminish it</b>; dark, very difficult times</li><li class=\"d0\">But if <b>trained by it</b> - shaped and formed - yields <b>fruit of righteousness</b></li><li class=\"d1\">transformation in our life, <b>more than worth</b> the unpleasantness</li><li class=\"d0\">Hard times <b>don't last forever</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"that's the <b>promise of the resurrection</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">received well → fruit later that is worth what we endure</li></ul>"},{"t":1634.74,"lv":3,"h":"12:12-13 - Hands that hang down","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>12:12-13</b>: strengthen the <b>hands which hang down</b> and feeble knees; make straight paths so what is lame <b>may not be dislocated, but healed</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Kind of an odd metaphor\" - again about <b>how we receive</b> hardship</li><li class=\"d0\">Hands down, feeble knees = someone who's been <b>striving and now weakening</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Distance runner whose <b>hip starts to hurt</b> - two ways it goes</li><li class=\"d1\">adapt and struggle through → <b>healed</b> after discomfort</li><li class=\"d1\">or <b>dislocated</b> - can't run at all</li><li class=\"d0\">Metaphor for how we receive pain and difficulty</li><li class=\"d1\">meant as <b>encouragement</b>: strengthen ourselves, <b>push on</b></li><li class=\"d1\">rather than giving in and <b>being broken by it</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1740.97,"lv":2,"h":"12:14 - Pursue peace and holiness","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>12:14</b>: pursue <b>peace with all people</b> and <b>holiness</b>, without which no one will see the Lord</li><li class=\"d0\">Now the <b>practical</b> side: how do I strengthen myself?</li><li class=\"d1\">he begins giving <b>practical examples</b> of strengthening ourselves in struggle</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Peace with all people</b></li><li class=\"d1\">suffering can make us <b>angry and bitter</b>, lash out, break <b>love for neighbor</b></li><li class=\"d1\">notice <b>\"pursue\"</b> - can't force others to be at peace; they must participate</li><li class=\"d1\">pursuing = not lashing out, not blaming, not taking out anger on others</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Holiness, without which no one will see the Lord</b></li><li class=\"d1\">again the <b>glorious appearing</b>, Christ's return, standing at his judgment seat with a clear conscience</li><li class=\"d0\">Why paired? Peace = <b>others</b>; holiness = <b>ourselves</b></li><li class=\"d1\">suffering can drive us to lose <b>personal holiness</b> - anger, bitterness, hate expressed as sinful actions</li><li class=\"d1\">\"acting out in different ways\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Pursue both → not going down the other road</li></ul>"},{"t":1949.91,"lv":2,"h":"12:15 - Root of bitterness","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>12:15</b>: look carefully lest anyone <b>fall short of the grace of God</b>, lest any <b>root of bitterness</b> spring up and defile many</li><li class=\"d0\">Another preaching habit: says the same thing <b>two or three different ways</b></li><li class=\"d1\">last verse = positive (what to pursue); this = <b>negative</b>, the mirror image</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Fall short of grace</b> ↔ not pursuing holiness and seeing the Lord</li><li class=\"d1\">about people <b>striving to see the Lord</b> - \"sorry Calvinists\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Root of bitterness</b> ↔ not pursuing peace</li><li class=\"d0\">In OT, sin is spoken of like a <b>disease</b> - this is how it's <b>contagious</b></li><li class=\"d1\">I suffer, bitterness takes root, I lash out and am <b>unkind and spiteful</b></li><li class=\"d1\">affects their mood, their view of the world, <b>their other relationships</b></li><li class=\"d1\">spreads through a <b>church, workplace, family</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Shows what happens if we don't <b>make a point to pursue peace</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2103.89,"lv":2,"h":"12:16 - Esau, fornicator and profane","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>12:16</b>: lest any <b>fornicator or profane person like Esau</b>, who for one morsel sold his birthright</li><li class=\"d0\">Esau already called fornicator and profane - Paul knows <b>the whole Jacob and Esau story</b> (see the <b>Romans 9</b> study)</li><li class=\"d1\">fornicator: married multiple <b>Canaanite wives</b>, upset his family</li><li class=\"d1\">profane = the opposite of <b>holy</b> - not pursuing personal holiness</li><li class=\"d0\">Example of the person in v. 15 - not pursuing peace or holiness</li><li class=\"d0\">Sold for one <b>bowl of pottage</b> his <b>birthright</b></li><li class=\"d1\">right of the firstborn - came out moments before twin <b>Jacob</b></li><li class=\"d1\">birthright = the <b>promises to Abraham</b>, his grandfather - \"the promise of salvation\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not that he lost his salvation</li><li class=\"d1\">story ends with Esau <b>reconciled</b>, seeking peace with Jacob</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul's example: he <b>traded it to gratify a desire in one moment</b></li><li class=\"d0\">A moment of suffering: the <b>passion of hunger</b>, weakness</li><li class=\"d1\">rather than strengthening himself, traded the <b>most valuable thing</b> for a bowl of soup</li></ul>"},{"t":2303.8,"lv":3,"h":"Why Esau is the example","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">We're prone to <b>make the same exchanges</b> without realizing it</li><li class=\"d0\">Under suffering, stress, difficulty we do <b>whatever we feel we need to get past that moment</b></li><li class=\"d1\">for many: <b>drugs, alcohol, sexual immorality</b>, other sins</li><li class=\"d0\">First: none of these solutions include <b>the soup</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not the greatest bowl of soup ever; Esau didn't never need to eat again</li><li class=\"d1\">solved nothing - but going down that road <b>gave up everything</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Parallel to Christ: <b>\"what does it profit a man to gain the whole world and lose his soul?\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">the most precious thing must be <b>knowledge of God, salvation, becoming like Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">don't trade him for what we need <b>right now</b> - all sufferings <b>have an end</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"if we're in Christ, we're capable of <b>losing everything</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2442.43,"lv":3,"h":"12:17 - No place for repentance, though sought with tears","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Second half of Christ's saying: <b>\"what will a man give in exchange for his soul?\"</b> = Esau finding no room for repentance</li><li class=\"d0\">Not saying Esau couldn't repent or be saved - Genesis shows he did</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Sought it with tears\"</b> = the actual story</li><li class=\"d1\">Rebecca conspired with Jacob, <b>fleece on his arms</b>, to get the firstborn blessing from <b>Isaac</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Jacob received it - only because <b>Esau had given it to him</b></li><li class=\"d1\">then Esau in his father's tent began to <b>weep</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Oh man, that was stupid. I made a <b>bad bargain</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">too late to get the birthright back; Isaac: <b>\"I've already given it to your brother\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Theme through Hebrews: at Christ's <b>glorious appearing and judgment seat</b> there's a point where it's <b>too late - no takebacks</b></li><li class=\"d1\">at any given time repentance is open; God wants us to <b>return, be forgiven and healed</b></li><li class=\"d1\">eventually a <b>point of no return</b> - gained the whole world, sold your soul</li><li class=\"d1\">nothing to give to buy it back, like <b>Esau</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2600.73,"lv":2,"h":"12:18-21 - Mount Sinai","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>12:18-21</b>: you have not come to the mountain that may be touched, burned with fire, blackness, darkness, tempest, trumpet, voice of words</li><li class=\"d0\">Clear reference to <b>Mount Sinai</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Israel out of Egypt, through the wilderness, <b>first Pentecost</b>, to receive the <b>covenant</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Descriptors recall it</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>physical mountain</b> in the desert, fire at the top, <b>clouds, storm, thunder, lightning</b></li><li class=\"d1\">trumpet and a <b>voice</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Israel freaked out - in God's holiness you grasp <b>how unholy we are</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"Don't let God talk to us\" - <b>we needed an intermediary</b>, Moses go up for us</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Beast touching the mountain</b> stoned or shot with an arrow</li><li class=\"d1\">your goat wanders onto the mountain → killed; can't go onto the mountain to get it, so <b>from a distance</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Even <b>Moses</b>: \"exceedingly afraid and trembling\"</li><li class=\"d1\">his own <b>sinfulness before God's holiness</b></li><li class=\"d0\">The Torah's terms for the first Pentecost, when Israel became a people and got the <b>old covenant</b></li><li class=\"d1\">old/new covenant comparison again and again in Hebrews; <b>how high the stakes were</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2785.21,"lv":2,"h":"12:22-24 - Mount Zion and the heavenly Jerusalem","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"That's not the mountain we've come to\" - <b>12:22</b>: <b>Mount Zion</b>, city of the living God, <b>heavenly Jerusalem</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Even more serious - <b>where God dwells</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not the physical temple in Jerusalem - contrast with a <b>mountain you can touch</b></li><li class=\"d1\">earthly sanctuary was an <b>image of the heavenly sanctuary</b></li><li class=\"d1\">what <b>only Moses</b> glimpsed on the mountain top - <b>we've come up the mountain</b>, not at the base terrified</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Innumerable company of angels</b> - where God is, angels are</li><li class=\"d1\">recalls the <b>divine council</b> language at the start of Hebrews</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>General assembly and church of the firstborn registered in heaven</b> - synonyms: congregation, assembly, gathering</li><li class=\"d1\">all the <b>heavenly, divine beings</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>God the judge of all</b> - God the Father</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Spirits of just men made perfect</b> - the saints of Heb. 11 made perfect <b>with us</b></li><li class=\"d1\">glancing reference to the <b>harrowing of Hades</b></li><li class=\"d1\">now not just angels but <b>human saints</b> part of the congregation</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Jesus, mediator of the new covenant</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2949.17,"lv":3,"h":"Blood of sprinkling that speaks better than Abel","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">New covenant = <b>blood of sprinkling</b></li><li class=\"d1\">at the base of Sinai they were <b>sprinkled with blood</b>, the oath-taking</li><li class=\"d1\">now the <b>blood of Christ</b> brings us into the new covenant</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Better than Abel</b></li><li class=\"d1\">after Cain killed Abel: \"Am I my brother's keeper?\" - <b>Abel's blood cries out from the ground</b></li><li class=\"d1\">cried for <b>vengeance</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christ's blood doesn't cry for vengeance on those who killed him, <b>including us</b></li><li class=\"d1\">cries for <b>mercy, forgiveness, healing, purification</b></li><li class=\"d0\">We've approached something much greater</li><li class=\"d1\">brought in as only a few OT prophets were - into the <b>family, household, city, dwelling, assembly of God</b></li></ul>"},{"t":3027.83,"lv":2,"h":"12:25 - Do not refuse him who speaks","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>12:25</b>: see that you do not refuse him who speaks; if they did not escape who refused him on earth, <b>much more shall we not</b> if we turn away from him who speaks from heaven</li><li class=\"d0\">At the base of the mountain, blood sprinkled: <b>\"Everything God commanded we will do\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"we know darn well they didn't do none of it\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Did not escape</b> = that whole generation died in the desert</li><li class=\"d1\">minus <b>Moses, Joshua, Caleb</b></li><li class=\"d1\">took the oath, sprinkled with blood, turned back → <b>consequences</b></li><li class=\"d0\">We receive a much greater word - <b>Christ himself</b>, greater blessing, calling</li><li class=\"d1\">if we turn back, don't remain faithful → <b>how much worse</b></li></ul>"},{"t":3122.13,"lv":3,"h":"Hebrews and \"once saved, always saved\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Another one of those <b>sorry Calvinist</b> passages\" all through Hebrews</li><li class=\"d0\">Whole thrust: speaking to <b>Christians</b> - don't turn back, fall away, grow weak, break under pressure</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Perseverance of the saints</b> / once saved always saved - <b>not compatible</b> with Hebrews</li><li class=\"d1\">no need for the homily if Paul believed it</li><li class=\"d1\">a Calvinist Paul: the elect will be saved; those who wash out <b>weren't elect</b>, can't be talked into salvation</li><li class=\"d0\">Calvinist reply: \"we don't know who the elect are, so evangelize everyone\"</li><li class=\"d1\">but Hebrews is <b>not an evangelistic homily</b> - not a public-square sermon, it's to Christians</li><li class=\"d0\">Central point over and over: as Israelites fell away, <b>Christians can fall away</b></li><li class=\"d1\">legitimately received Christ, the <b>heavenly Spirit</b>, the <b>sacraments</b></li><li class=\"d1\">suffer <b>much worse condemnation</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"That's simply what Hebrews very clearly says\"</li></ul>"},{"t":3248.82,"lv":2,"h":"12:26-27 - Yet once more I shake","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>12:26</b>: his voice then <b>shook the earth</b> (Sinai - the mountain and ground shook); now he promises <b>\"yet once more I shake not only the earth but also heaven\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>12:27</b>: \"yet once more\" = <b>removal of things that are made</b>, so the unshakable remain</li><li class=\"d0\">Image: <b>panning for gold</b></li></ul>"},{"t":3279.42,"lv":3,"h":"Panning for gold","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Grew up in <b>Southern California</b>; state history in <b>3rd or 4th grade</b></li><li class=\"d1\">field trip to <b>pan for gold</b> in the gold rush unit</li><li class=\"d0\">Flat pan, scoop silt from the river bottom, <b>shake</b></li><li class=\"d1\">lighter dirt falls out; <b>gold is heavy</b> so it remains and stands out</li><li class=\"d1\">(no real gold for us kids)</li><li class=\"d0\">Any sifting - \"draining pasta\" - shaking to <b>winnow out</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Applied: a <b>last judgment</b> is coming</li><li class=\"d1\">the whole creation <b>shaken</b>; a lot falls away, <b>gold remains</b></li><li class=\"d1\">hence the importance of <b>standing firm, remaining faithful</b></li></ul>"},{"t":3388.34,"lv":2,"h":"12:28 - A kingdom that cannot be shaken","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>12:28</b>: receiving a <b>kingdom that cannot be shaken</b>; let us have grace to serve God acceptably with <b>reverence and godly fear</b></li><li class=\"d0\">The promise we receive <b>stays</b> when all the things of this world are shaken away</li><li class=\"d1\">the unimportant things, distractions, what we'd trade faithfulness for - <b>\"that bowl of soup\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Focus on <b>receiving grace by which we serve God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Orthodox: <b>grace is God acting</b> in the world</li><li class=\"d1\">grace = means by which we serve: <b>cooperate with what God is doing</b> → serving him <b>and</b> receiving grace, transformed</li><li class=\"d0\">Example: God is establishing <b>peace</b> in the world</li><li class=\"d1\">seek peace with those around us → <b>participate</b>, transformed</li><li class=\"d1\">\"a little more <b>gold</b>, a little less <b>dross</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Across the spectrum: <b>peace, love, justice/righteousness, joy, faithfulness</b></li><li class=\"d1\">act in accord with God → transformed to be <b>ready to enter the kingdom</b></li></ul>"},{"t":3522.15,"lv":2,"h":"12:29 - Our God is a consuming fire","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>12:29</b>: <b>\"our God is a consuming fire\"</b> - similar but different metaphor</li><li class=\"d0\">Sifting → <b>purifying metal</b></li><li class=\"d1\">find the nugget panning, put it in a <b>furnace</b>, melt it down</li><li class=\"d1\">dirt, garbage, junk <b>burns away</b>; <b>pure gold</b> left</li><li class=\"d0\">Judgment is <b>both a threat and a promise</b></li><li class=\"d1\">threat: traded the gold God gave for <b>garbage</b> → <b>burnt up</b>, left with nothing when sifted</li><li class=\"d1\">promise: if living in grace, serving God, faithful → only the <b>garbage still in us burns away</b></li><li class=\"d1\">the <b>pure gold</b> we struggled for, God produced in us, <b>remains forever</b></li></ul>"}]});
